    correctOptionIds: ["a", "b", "i", "j"],
    answerType: "multi",
    explanation: "RDS automated backups run daily during a backup window and, together with transaction logs, allow point-in-time recovery for a retention period of 0 to 35 days. Manual snapshots are user-initiated, persist until deleted, and can be copied to other Regions or shared with other accounts, making them suitable for long-term retention and disaster recovery.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "The automated backup retention period is configurable up to 35 days and supports restoring to any second within it.",
      b: "Manual snapshots are not subject to the retention period and survive instance deletion.",
      c: "Automated backups are deleted after the retention period (default 7 days when created via console).",
      d: "Snapshots can be restored to any AZ in the Region and copied to other Regions.",
      e: "Automated backups are, by definition, taken automatically by RDS.",
      f: "Automated backups are stored durably in Amazon S3, independent of the DB instance's storage.",
      g: "The 35-day limit applies to automated backup retention; manual snapshots persist until you delete them.",
      h: "Point-in-time recovery always restores to a new DB instance with a new endpoint; the original is left unchanged.",
      i: "Copying snapshots to a second Region keeps a restorable backup available if the primary Region becomes unavailable.",
      j: "Sharing a manual snapshot lets another account restore its own copy of the database, for example in a separate backup or test account.",
    },
