    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "Amazon API Gateway offers REST APIs, HTTP APIs, and WebSocket APIs. REST and HTTP APIs follow the request/response model, where the client must initiate every exchange. A WebSocket API maintains a persistent connection and lets the backend send messages to connected clients at any time via the @connections callback, which suits chat, live dashboards, and multiplayer games. Routes such as $connect, $disconnect, and custom routes map to Lambda or other integrations.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. WebSocket APIs provide persistent, bidirectional communication with server push.",
      b: "REST APIs are request/response; caching does not enable server push.",
      c: "HTTP APIs are also request/response; CORS only governs cross-origin browser calls.",
      d: "A private REST API restricts access to a VPC but is still request/response.",
      e: "REST APIs are request-response; the server cannot push messages to the client over a persistent connection.",
      f: "HTTP APIs handle stateless request-response traffic; they do not maintain two-way connections.",
      g: "Caching stores responses to reduce backend calls; it does not enable server push.",
      h: "Endpoint type affects where requests enter; it does not provide bidirectional messaging.",
      i: "Correct. A WebSocket API keeps a persistent two-way connection, routes such as $connect and $disconnect invoke Lambda, and the backend can push messages to clients.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apigateway-websocket-api.html",
    referenceLabel: "WebSocket APIs in API Gateway",
