    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "A default public IPv4 address is released when an instance stops and a new one is assigned on start. An Elastic IP address is a static public IPv4 address allocated to your account that you associate with an instance or network interface; it persists across stop/start and can be re-associated with a different instance in minutes, which also makes it useful for failover. Since 2024 AWS charges a small hourly fee for every public IPv4 address, including Elastic IPs, whether attached or not.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. Elastic IPs are static, account-owned public IPv4 addresses that survive stop/start and can be remapped.",
      b: "Private addresses are not reachable from customers' networks over the internet.",
      c: "CloudFront is for caching web content and does not provide a fixed IP for an arbitrary licensing protocol.",
      d: "Instance size has no effect on whether a public IP is retained.",
      e: "The public DNS hostname is derived from the public IP and changes along with it on stop/start.",
      f: "ALB DNS names resolve to changing IP addresses, so customers could not whitelist a fixed IP.",
      g: "A NAT gateway handles outbound traffic for private instances; it does not give the licensing server a fixed inbound address.",
      h: "Instance metadata reports the current addresses; it does not make them persistent.",
      i: "Correct. This describes an Elastic IP address, which stays the same across stop/start and can be reassociated with a replacement instance.",
    },
