    correctOptionIds: ["e", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "AWS PrivateLink lets a provider publish a service that sits behind a Network Load Balancer (or Gateway Load Balancer) as a VPC endpoint service. Each consumer creates an interface VPC endpoint in their own VPC that gets private IP addresses, and traffic flows over the AWS network without VPC peering, route table changes, or overlapping CIDR concerns. The provider can require acceptance of each connection and restrict which principals may connect, keeping its network isolated. This is the same mechanism AWS uses to expose its own services privately.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Peering does not scale to dozens of customers and exposes both networks to each other.",
      b: "An internet gateway sends traffic over the public internet, which the requirement forbids.",
      c: "Transit Gateway connects networks fully; it is for your own or trusted networks, not multi-tenant customer isolation.",
      d: "VPNs traverse the internet (encrypted) and require per-customer configuration.",
      e: "Correct. PrivateLink endpoint services give each customer a private interface endpoint to the provider's NLB.",
      f: "Edge-optimized API Gateway endpoints are public, internet-facing endpoints.",
      g: "Global Accelerator provides public anycast IPs and uses the internet for the last hop.",
      h: "CloudFront distributes content from the edge over the internet; it does not create private customer connectivity.",
      i: "Correct. An endpoint service fronted by the NLB is AWS PrivateLink; each customer creates an interface endpoint in its own VPC and traffic stays on the AWS network.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/privatelink-share-your-services.html",
    referenceLabel: "Share your services through AWS PrivateLink",
