    id: "tech311",
    domain: "cloud-technology-and-services",
    text: "An engineering firm runs a tightly coupled computational fluid dynamics simulation across 64 Amazon EC2 instances that exchange data constantly using MPI. Job run time is dominated by network latency between the nodes. The firm wants AWS to place the instances physically close together inside a single Availability Zone to minimize latency and maximize throughput. Which EC2 feature should the firm use?",
    options: [
      { id: "a", text: "A cluster placement group" },
      { id: "b", text: "A spread placement group" },
      { id: "c", text: "A partition placement group" },
      { id: "d", text: "An Auto Scaling group with a step scaling policy" },
      { id: "e", text: "EC2 Dedicated Hosts" },
      { id: "f", text: "An EC2 Capacity Reservation" },
      { id: "g", text: "Enhanced networking on an Amazon Elastic Beanstalk environment" },
      { id: "h", text: "An AWS Global Accelerator endpoint group" },
      { id: "i", text: "A placement group with the cluster strategy" },
    ],
    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "A cluster placement group packs instances close together inside one Availability Zone so they get low-latency, high-throughput network connectivity, which is exactly what tightly coupled HPC and MPI workloads need. Spread and partition placement groups do the opposite: they separate instances across hardware to reduce correlated failures for workloads such as distributed databases.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. Cluster placement groups co-locate instances in one AZ for the lowest inter-node latency.",
      b: "Spread placement groups put each instance on distinct underlying hardware to reduce correlated failures, which increases rather than reduces distance.",
      c: "Partition placement groups spread instance groups across logical partitions with separate racks; they are designed for Hadoop, Cassandra, and Kafka, not latency-sensitive MPI.",
      d: "Auto Scaling policies control how many instances run, not where they are physically placed.",
      e: "Dedicated Hosts give a physical server for licensing and compliance reasons; they do not co-locate a 64-node cluster for low latency.",
      f: "Capacity Reservations guarantee capacity in an AZ but say nothing about network proximity.",
      g: "Elastic Beanstalk is for deploying web applications; enhanced networking helps a single instance but does not control placement.",
      h: "Global Accelerator improves internet-to-AWS routing for users, not east-west traffic between compute nodes.",
      i: "Correct. Choosing the cluster strategy packs the instances onto closely connected hardware in one AZ for the lowest latency.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/placement-groups.html",
    referenceLabel: "Placement groups for your Amazon EC2 instances",
    consoleUrl: "https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2/home#PlacementGroups:",
    consoleLabel: "EC2 Placement Groups",
    diagram: "flowchart LR\n  PG[Cluster placement group in one AZ] --> N1[Node 1]\n  PG --> N2[Node 2]\n  PG --> N3[Node 64]\n  N1 <--> N2\n  N2 <--> N3\n  N1 <--> N3",
